      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-07-09">Published July 9, 2026</time></p>

      <div set:html={`
<p>Google's NotebookLM — the AI-powered research assistant that converts documents into searchable knowledge bases with auto-generated podcasts, study guides, and interactive Q&A — just gained native <strong>EPUB support</strong>. As first reported by multiple outlets including Android Authority, XDA, MakeUseOf, and gHacks in early July 2026, NotebookLM can now directly ingest EPUB files, making it an indispensable tool for students, researchers, and anyone who reads digital books.</p> <p>Until this update, NotebookLM supported Google Docs, PDFs, and web URLs as source documents. EPUB was a notable gap, since EPUB is the most widely supported open ebook format used by Kobo, PocketBook, Google Play Books, Apple Books, and most library lending platforms like Libby and OverDrive. The update quietly closes that gap and opens up an entirely new use case: AI-assisted book reading.</p> <p>This guide explains what NotebookLM's EPUB support means, how to upload and analyze your ebook files, and best practices for getting the most out of the combination.</p> <h2>What Is Google NotebookLM?</h2> <p>NotebookLM (short for Notebook Language Model) is Google's AI-powered note-taking and research tool. Launched in 2023 and continuously upgraded through 2026, it allows users to upload source documents and then query an LLM — now powered by Google's Gemini 3.5 model — against those documents. The core idea is that the AI answers questions <em>only from your sources</em>, not from the open internet, making it ideal for academic research, legal review, and deep reading.</p> <p>Key features include automatic source summaries, interactive Q&A, study guide generation, and the popular Audio Overviews feature that turns documents into AI-hosted podcast-style conversations. The July 2026 update brings all of these features to EPUB files alongside the existing PDF and document support.</p> <h2>Why EPUB Support Matters</h2> <p>The addition of native EPUB support transforms NotebookLM from a research-tool-for-documents into a legitimate book-reading assistant. Here is why this matters:</p> <ul> <li><strong>EPUB is the standard for ebooks.</strong> Unlike PDF (which is a page-layout format) or AZW3 (which is Amazon-proprietary), EPUB is an open, reflowable format designed for reading. Books in EPUB form can adapt to any screen size, include accessibility features, and are the default format for libraries and most independent publishers.</li> <li><strong>EPUB files are smaller than PDFs.</strong> A 300-page novel in EPUB is typically 500KB to 2MB, compared to 10MB to 30MB as a PDF. This makes working with multiple books in NotebookLM faster and more efficient.</li> <li><strong>EPUB preserves metadata.</strong> Chapter structure, headings, tables of contents, and semantic markup are all present in EPUB files, which means NotebookLM can index them more intelligently than it could with scanned PDFs.</li> <li><strong>The existing ebook ecosystem is enormous.</strong> Standard Ebooks, Project Gutenberg, Internet Archive, and many library lending platforms all distribute in EPUB format. A typical researcher may have hundreds of EPUB books on their hard drive. Those books can now become searchable AI research collections.</li> </ul> <h2>How to Upload EPUB Files to NotebookLM</h2> <p>Using EPUB files in NotebookLM is straightforward. The process mirrors the existing document upload flow:</p> <h3>Step 1: Open NotebookLM</h3> <p>Navigate to <a href="https://notebooklm.google" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">notebooklm.google</a> and sign in with your Google account. NotebookLM is free to use with a Google account, though there are daily source limits depending on the plan tier.</p> <h3>Step 2: Create or Open a Notebook</h3> <p>A notebook in NotebookLM is a project-level container for related source files. If you are studying a single book, create one notebook for it. If you are doing a literature review with ten books, create a notebook for the project and add all of them as sources.</p> <h3>Step 3: Add EPUB as a Source</h3> <p>Click the source icon in the left sidebar (or the "+ Add source" button depending on the interface version). Select "Upload from device" and choose your EPUB file. Alternatively, if the EPUB is stored in Google Drive, select Google Drive from the source menu and navigate to the file.</p> <p>NotebookLM supports up to 50 sources per notebook, with a maximum of 500,000 total words across all sources (roughly equivalent to 5 to 10 full-length books, depending on density).</p> <h3>Step 4: Wait for Processing</h3> <p>After uploading, NotebookLM processes the EPUB file. The processing time depends on the book's length. A 300-page novel takes 10 to 20 seconds; a 1,000-page academic text may take 60 to 90 seconds. You will see a status indicator next to the source while it processes.</p> <h3>Step 5: Explore the Results</h3> <p>Once processed, NotebookLM generates an automatic summary of the book, a list of key topics, and suggested questions. You can click any generated question to get an answer grounded in the book's content, or type your own questions in the chat interface.</p> <h2>How to Export Your Books to EPUB for NotebookLM</h2> <p>If your ebook collection is scattered across different formats — Kindle AZW3, PDF, MOBI, or even physical scans — you may need to convert them to EPUB before uploading to NotebookLM. This is where converter-epub.com comes in.</p> <p>Use converter-epub.com to convert any document or ebook file to EPUB format:</p> <ol> <li>Go to <a href="https://converter-epub.com">converter-epub.com</a></li> <li>Upload your file (PDF, DOCX, MOBI, AZW3, TXT, HTML, and many other formats are supported)</li> <li>Click convert — the tool runs entirely in your browser with no upload to external servers</li> <li>Download the clean EPUB file</li> <li>Upload it to NotebookLM</li> </ol> <p>This workflow is particularly useful for Kindle users who have decades of Amazon-purchased content in AZW3 or KFX format. Amazon now allows <a href="/blog/amazon-drm-free-kindle-books-2026/">DRM-free downloads of many Kindle books as EPUB</a>, but converting your existing library requires a separate step.</p> <h2>Best Ways to Use NotebookLM With EPUB Books</h2> <p>Once your EPUB file is in NotebookLM, the real value begins. Here are the most effective ways to use the combination:</p> <h3>Book Summaries in Seconds</h3> <p>The most obvious use case: upload a book and get a detailed chapter-by-chapter summary. NotebookLM's source guide feature automatically generates a summary, key topics, and a list of important concepts. This is invaluable for textbooks, academic monographs, and dense non-fiction where you need to understand the structure before deep reading.</p> <h3>Interactive Q&A With Your Book</h3> <p>Instead of re-reading hundreds of pages to find a specific argument or data point, simply ask NotebookLM. Questions like "What were the three main causes of the 2008 financial crisis according to Chapter 4?" or "What methodology did the author use in the field study?" are answered directly from the EPUB source. The AI cites specific passages, letting you click through to verify the original text.</p> <h3>Study Guide Generation</h3> <p>NotebookLM can generate study guides, briefing documents, and timelines from your uploaded EPUBs. For students reading assigned textbooks in EPUB format, this turns each book into an automatically generated study session. The study guide feature extracts key terms, generates practice questions, and organizes content by chapter.</p> <h3>Audio Overviews (AI Podcasts)</h3> <p>One of NotebookLM's most popular features is Audio Overviews — automatically generated podcast-style conversations between two AI hosts that discuss your source material. With EPUB support, you can now turn a book into a listenable audio discussion. The AI hosts do not simply read the book aloud; they discuss, analyze, and debate its content in a natural conversational format. This is a compelling way to absorb a book during a commute or workout.</p> <h3>Cross-Book Research</h3> <p>The true power of NotebookLM emerges when you upload multiple EPUB books to a single notebook. A literature review that previously required manual cross-referencing of five separate books becomes a single searchable knowledge base. You can ask questions that span all sources: "Which authors agree on the effectiveness of cognitive behavioral therapy, and which disagree? Show me the evidence from each book."</p> <h2>Tips for Best Results With EPUB in NotebookLM</h2> <p>Not all EPUB files are created equal. The quality of NotebookLM's analysis depends heavily on the EPUB's internal structure. Follow these tips for the best results:</p> <table> <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Affects</th><th>Recommendation</th></tr> <tr><td>Semantic markup</td><td>Chapter recognition, TOC generation</td><td>Use well-structured EPUBs with proper <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> heading hierarchy. Books from Standard Ebooks or major publishers are ideal.</td></tr> <tr><td>Clean formatting</td><td>Q&A accuracy, summary quality</td><td>Avoid EPUBs with inline CSS that overrides semantic structure. Run messy EPUBs through Calibre's Polish tool before uploading.</td></tr> <tr><td>Complete metadata</td><td>Source identification</td><td>Ensure the EPUB has a proper title, author, and language set in its OPF metadata. This helps NotebookLM identify the source correctly.</td></tr> <tr><td>Image-heavy content</td><td>AI comprehension</td><td>NotebookLM analyzes text content, not image content. Image-heavy EPUBs (comics, illustrated textbooks) will have limited analysis quality unless the images have good alt text.</td></tr> <tr><td>Proper encoding</td><td>Character rendering</td><td>Ensure the EPUB uses UTF-8 encoding. Older EPUBs with non-standard encodings may display garbled characters in NotebookLM's interface.</td></tr> </table> <h2>Limitations to Know</h2> <p>NotebookLM's EPUB support is powerful but has important limitations that you should understand before relying on it for critical research:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Word count limits.</strong> Each notebook has a 500,000-word source limit across all documents. A single dense academic book may consume 150,000 to 200,000 words of that limit. Plan your notebooks accordingly — one notebook per book for long works, or one notebook per topic for shorter works.</li> <li><strong>No EPUB export.</strong> NotebookLM can generate study guides, notes, and Q&A transcripts, but it cannot export back to EPUB format. If you want to save NotebookLM's output as a portable document, you need to copy it manually or use a browser extension.</li> <li><strong>No DRM support.</strong> NotebookLM cannot ingest DRM-protected EPUB files. You must remove DRM first (where legally permissible) or use DRM-free sources like Standard Ebooks, Project Gutenberg, or Amazon's newly <a href="/blog/amazon-drm-free-kindle-books-2026/">DRM-free Kindle titles</a>.</li> <li><strong>Internet connection required.</strong> NotebookLM is a cloud service. All processing happens on Google's servers. If you need offline reading or analysis, a local EPUB reader or tool like <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre</a> is a better choice for the reading experience itself.</li> <li><strong>Accuracy caveats.</strong> While NotebookLM is source-grounded and less prone to hallucination than general-purpose LLMs, the AI can still misinterpret passages or miss context. Always verify critical claims against the original EPUB text, especially for technical or nuanced subject matter.</li> </ul> <h2>NotebookLM vs. Other EPUB Reading and Research Tools</h2> <p>How does NotebookLM compare to existing tools for reading and analyzing EPUB files? The key difference is that NotebookLM is an <em>analysis</em> tool, not a <em>reading</em> tool. It complements, rather than replaces, traditional EPUB readers.</p> <table> <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Best For</th><th>EPUB Support</th><th>AI Features</th></tr> <tr><td>NotebookLM</td><td>Research, analysis, cross-referencing</td><td>Native (July 2026)</td><td>Q&A, summaries, Audio Overviews, study guides</td></tr> <tr><td>Calibre</td><td>Library management, format conversion, metadata editing</td><td>Full read/write/convert</td><td>None</td></tr> <tr><td>KOReader</td><td>On-device reading on E Ink and other devices</td><td>Full reader with annotations</td><td>None</td></tr> <tr><td>Thorium Reader</td><td>Desktop EPUB reading with accessibility</td><td>Full reader with OPDS support</td><td>None</td></tr> <tr><td>ChatPDF-style tools</td><td>Document Q&A (general)</td><td>Varies</td><td>Basic Q&A, no Audio Overviews</td></tr> </table> <p>The ideal workflow for a researcher who reads EPUB books: use <a href="/">converter-epub.com</a> to prepare any non-EPUB content, upload the result to NotebookLM for analysis and Q&A, and read the actual book on your preferred EPUB reader (KOreader on E Ink, Thorium on desktop, or a dedicated app like the ones in our <a href="/blog/best-epub-reader-apps-2026/">best EPUB reader apps guide</a>).</p> <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"> <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Is NotebookLM free to use with EPUB files?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. NotebookLM is free with a Google account. The free tier includes 50 sources per notebook and a 500,000-word limit. Google also offers NotebookLM Plus for Google Workspace subscribers with higher limits and priority access to new features.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I upload DRM-protected EPUB files from Amazon or Kobo?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>No. NotebookLM cannot process DRM-protected EPUB files. You need DRM-free EPUBs. Amazon now offers many Kindle books as DRM-free EPUB downloads through its updated KDP policy, and services like Standard Ebooks and Project Gutenberg provide entirely DRM-free content. For legally owned books with DRM, you may need to remove DRM first where permitted by local law.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">How many EPUB files can I upload to NotebookLM?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Each notebook supports up to 50 source documents, including EPUB files, PDFs, web URLs, and Google Docs. The total word count across all sources cannot exceed 500,000 words. For a typical 300-page novel (80,000 to 100,000 words), you can fit 5 to 6 books in a single notebook. For denser academic books (150,000+ words), plan for 2 to 3 books per notebook.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can NotebookLM read EPUB files aloud?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Not in the traditional text-to-speech sense. NotebookLM's Audio Overviews feature generates a conversational podcast-style discussion about the book's content — two AI hosts debate, analyze, and summarize the material. It is not designed for page-by-page reading aloud. For traditional EPUB text-to-speech, use a dedicated EPUB reader app like Thorium Reader or a voice assistant app on your phone.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">How does NotebookLM EPUB support compare to reading on a Kindle or Kobo?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>They serve completely different purposes. A Kindle or Kobo is optimized for the <em>act of reading</em> — long sessions on an E Ink screen with no distractions. NotebookLM is optimized for <em>analyzing what you read</em> — extracting key points, answering specific questions, and connecting ideas across multiple books. The best approach is to use both: read the book on your e-reader and use NotebookLM as a research companion.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I convert my Kindle books to EPUB for NotebookLM?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. Amazon now offers DRM-free EPUB downloads for many Kindle books through KDP. For older purchases in AZW3 or KFX format, convert them first using converter-epub.com. Simply upload the file, select EPUB as the output format, and download the converted version. The conversion runs entirely in your browser — nothing is uploaded to any server.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does NotebookLM work with EPUB files in languages other than English?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. NotebookLM supports multiple languages through Google's Gemini 3.5 model. The quality of analysis depends on the language, with strongest support for English, followed by European languages (Spanish, French, German, Portuguese, Italian), and then Asian languages. Audio Overviews are currently most reliable in English, though Google has been expanding language support throughout 2026.</p> </div> </div>
      `}/>
    </article>
  </main>
